{"causes": ["The political fragmentation of China during the Warring States period fostered a desperate search for unifying philosophical and cosmological frameworks.", "The intellectual ferment of the Jixia Academy in Qi provided a unique environment for diverse philosophical schools to interact and synthesize ideas.", "Pre-existing traditions of Yin-Yang cosmology and early elemental theories offered raw material for Zou Yan's systematic integration and expansion."], "effects": ["Zou Yan's Five Phases theory became a cornerstone for legitimizing imperial rule, particularly for the Qin and Han dynasties.", "His cosmological ideas deeply influenced Chinese statecraft, calendrics, divination, and even traditional Chinese medicine for centuries.", "The Yin-Yang and Five Phases framework provided a comprehensive interpretive lens for understanding natural and historical cycles in East Asian thought."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "hundred-schools-of-thought", "sourceName": "Hundred Schools of Thought", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "zou-yan", "targetName": "Zou Yan", "context": "Zou Yan was a representative thinker within the intellectual flourishing of the Hundred Schools of Thought during the Warring States period."}, {"sourceSlug": "jixia-academy", "sourceName": "Jixia Academy", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "zou-yan", "targetName": "Zou Yan", "context": "The vibrant intellectual environment of the Jixia Academy in Qi provided the context for Zou Yan's philosophical development."}, {"sourceSlug": "zou-yan", "sourceName": "Zou Yan", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "qin-dynasty", "targetName": "Qin Dynasty", "context": "Zou Yan's Five Phases theory was adopted by the Qin Dynasty to legitimize its conquest and establishment of imperial rule around 221 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "zou-yan", "sourceName": "Zou Yan", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "han-dynasty", "targetName": "Han Dynasty", "context": "The Han Dynasty extensively utilized Zou Yan's cosmological framework for its imperial rituals, calendrical systems, and dynastic legitimacy from 206 BCE onwards."}, {"sourceSlug": "zou-yan", "sourceName": "Zou Yan", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-china", "targetName": "Ancient China", "context": "Zou Yan developed his philosophical system within the cultural and political landscape of ancient China during the Warring States period."}], "places": [{"name": "Linzi, China", "role": "Intellectual Center"}, {"name": "State of Qi, China", "role": "Patronage Region"}, {"name": "Ancient China", "role": "Geographic Scope"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Zou Yan", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "zou-yan"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q227022", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zou_Yan"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 8, "significanceNarrative": "Zou Yan profoundly shaped Chinese imperial ideology and governance for over two millennia through his synthesis of Yin-Yang and Five Phases theories, influencing the legitimacy and rituals of dynasties from Qin to Qing. His cosmological framework provided a comprehensive lens for understanding natural and historical cycles, impacting statecraft, calendrics, and even medicine.", "significanceCategory": "continental"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-15T09:11:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43697840692", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Zou Yan was a Chinese philosopher and spiritual writer of the Warring States-era. He was best known as the representative thinker of the Yin and Yang School during the Hundred Schools of Thought era in Chinese philosophy.", "newValue": "Zou Yan (c. 305–239 BCE), a central figure of the Jixia Academy, revolutionized Chinese cosmological and political thought during the tumultuous Warring States period. His profound synthesis of Yin-Yang principles with the Five Phases (Wuxing) theory provided a comprehensive framework for understanding natural phenomena, historical cycles, and dynastic legitimacy. This grand intellectual system profoundly influenced subsequent imperial ideology, establishing him as the foremost representative thinker of the Yin and Yang School.

Operating within the vibrant intellectual hub of the Jixia Academy in the state of Qi, Zou Yan developed a sophisticated system that mapped the cosmos onto human affairs. He posited that the Five Phases—Wood, Fire, Earth, Metal, Water—cyclically succeeded one another, each phase associated with a specific color, direction, season, and, crucially, a ruling dynasty. This theory explained the rise and fall of states, offering a predictive model for political change and providing a powerful justification for new regimes, a concept particularly appealing to ambitious rulers seeking to unify a fragmented China.

Zou Yan's cosmological framework, though often critiqued by later Confucian scholars for its deterministic aspects, became foundational to Chinese imperial thought. His Five Phases theory was adopted and adapted by the nascent Qin and subsequent Han dynasties to legitimize their rule, influencing everything from state rituals and official colors to calendrical reforms and the writing of historical narratives. The concept of a cyclical, divinely ordained mandate for rulers, deeply rooted in his ideas, persisted for millennia, shaping the very structure of Chinese political philosophy and governance."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-15T09:11:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43697840692", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "304 BCE"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "304 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The political fragmentation of China during the Warring States period fostered a desperate search for unifying philosophical and cosmological frameworks.", "The intellectual ferment of the Jixia Academy in Qi provided a unique environment for diverse philosophical schools to interact and synthesize ideas.", "Pre-existing traditions of Yin-Yang cosmology and early elemental theories offered raw material for Zou Yan's systematic integration and expansion."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-15T09:11:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43697840692", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "239 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "239 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Zou Yan's Five Phases theory became a cornerstone for legitimizing imperial rule, particularly for the Qin an